
After completing ALL work below, stop and hand control back to the parent loop. Do not run Shark status-transition commands yourself; report the recommended outcome in your final response so the parent can advance the workflow.

Review bug E07-F01 against the accepted repair plan, research, coverage index and all unaccepted predecessor changes. Bind review evidence to the complete candidate and plan revision. Before advancing an in-flight bug, inspect existing evidence and return to the earliest step with missing or invalid evidence; preserve code and history. Verify the approved plan content hash matches the current plan. Review the plan's single coverage index, which maps each obligation to its authoritative source, production caller or boundary, planned change, required assertions, and links to a separate mutable evidence record. Do not change the plan while reviewing its revision. Reference existing decisions; escalate conflicting authority to its owner. Keep all findings and route to the earliest invalidated stage. Diagnosis changes invalidate the plan and downstream evidence; material plan changes invalidate approval; candidate changes invalidate review and QA. Reuse unchanged valid evidence. Unavailable external evidence is blocked, not acceptance. For a structured success outcome, include exactly one outer `evidence` entry with `kind: bug_repair_plan`, `pointer` set to the project-relative plan file, and `summary` set to `sha256:<hex digest>` of the current plan bytes. Also include exactly one `bug_review_baseline` evidence entry whose `pointer` is the full commit hash of the review baseline declared in that plan. Independently confirm the baseline at plan review and carry the same value through development, review and QA; do not infer or substitute a later baseline. The parent validates these identities; it does not establish evidence quality by itself.

For a structured pass, retain the complete scope JSON produced by the deep-review capture in a project-relative ignored location such as `.shark/runs/`, and include exactly one outer evidence entry with `kind: bug_review_scope`, its project-relative `pointer`, and `summary: sha256:<hex digest>` of the retained scope bytes. The parent checks the scope and current source candidate before advancing. Keep the same scope artifact for QA.

Capture against the independently approved `bug_review_baseline` commit. Set `REVIEW_PLAN_SHA256` to the approved plan digest without its `sha256:` prefix; ensure the scope, every angle result, and the report carry that exact plan revision.

Also retain the generated deep-review report and include a digest-bound `bug_review_report` evidence reference in the same format. Include the generated candidate-bound decisions JSON as a digest-bound `bug_review_decisions` reference. For a native or dispatched six-angle review, retain the actual six angle-results JSON and include a digest-bound `bug_review_angles` reference. A comparable alternate-model CLI review uses its report and decisions without angle results. Every blocker needs an explicit matching refutation with an evidence-based reason for a pass. An incomplete review cannot pass.
Use review_plan for a valid plan whose approval is missing or stale; use needs_research for a causal contradiction, replan for a design omission, fail for a coding defect, and blocked for unavailable external evidence. Retain every finding; route to the earliest invalidated stage. For route_rework outcomes leave kickbacks empty.

Run the host-installed `shark-rider/skills/deep-review/SKILL.md` multi-agent procedure for this code-review gate, including its capability ladder and consolidation. The parent retains the entity lease and transitions; reviewers supply evidence only.


# Workflow: Code Review (Craft Review)

## Purpose

Review code changes for **craftsmanship** — DRY, SOLID, idiomatic patterns, standards compliance, complexity, reuse opportunities — and elevate quality with concrete, actionable feedback. Findings that don't block the task get returned as `non_blockers_to_triage` so they don't get lost.

## Phase Contract

This workflow does **craft review** only. It does NOT re-verify what other phases own:

| What this phase owns | What this phase HANDS OFF to QA |
|---|---|
| DRY / reuse opportunities | Spec fidelity (PRD ↔ implementation) |
| SOLID and architectural compliance | Acceptance criteria verification |
| Standards crosswalk (with citations) | Runtime wiring / call-site verification |
| Idiomatic language patterns | Frontend visual verification |
| Complexity, size, hotspots | Test execution (running pytest, lint, type-check) |
| Reuse search across codebase | E2E reachability |
| Tests **design** review (coverage, boundaries, missing cases) | Codex red-team for spec fidelity |

If you find yourself re-running tests or re-verifying ACs against the PRD, **stop** — that's QA's job. Trust the phase contract; if QA is doing its job, you don't need to re-do it.

A blocker found here halts the task; a non-blocker is returned in `non_blockers_to_triage` so the host can route it through the triage skill's decision tree (full contract in Step 12) — the finding may become a bug, task, question, change, or tech-debt depending on that classification, never tech-debt by default.

### Prompt-only changes

For embedded prompt, skill, template, or documentation-only changes, review
the changed wording against the specification and confirm bundle integrity:
templates render, includes resolve, and newly documented local file references
exist. Do not require a simulated decision table, wording mutation test, or
runtime caller-path trace unless the change alters deterministic runtime
behavior.

## Required inputs (validation)

If any critical input is missing, request it before proceeding. **Do not invent standards or fabricate citations.** If `coding_standards_path` is absent, every standards violation downgrades to opinion-level (nit).

## Review process

### Step 1: Scope sanity (60 seconds — not an AC re-check)

A 5-line gut check, not an AC re-verification:

- Diff touches files plausibly related to the task title.
- Diff size is plausible for the task description (flag if `diff_summary.lines_changed > 2000` — recommend splitting).
- No unrelated modifications.

If the diff doesn't plausibly match the task, **FAIL immediately and return** — something is wrong upstream.

### Step 2: Reuse pass (DRY — search before flagging duplication)

**Before** flagging duplication, search the codebase for existing implementations:

```bash
# Examples — adapt to the language and symbol style
grep -rn "<function-name>" --include="*.py" .
grep -rn "<class-name>" --include="*.ts" src/
rg "<concept>" --type-add 'src:*.{py,ts,tsx,go}' -tsrc
```

For each new utility / helper / abstraction the diff introduces, check whether the codebase already has one. If it does:

- Document the existing symbol with its `file:line`.
- Propose a diff-ready refactor showing how the new code would call it.

Findings: list each duplicated logic with the existing code's location, severity (blocker if a major reimplementation, non-blocker if a small parallel utility).

### Step 3: Standards crosswalk (required citations)

For each finding in this phase, cite the **specific section** of the project's standards docs that the code violates. **No citation = the finding is opinion, not a standards violation.**

For each violation: `{file:line} — {rule} — {coding-standards.md §X.Y "quoted clause"}`.

If the project has no standards section that covers a finding, note "no standards section covers this — opinion only" and downgrade to a nit.

### Step 4: SOLID & architecture compliance

Validate each modified file against:

- **Single Responsibility** — does each class/module have one clear job?
- **Open/Closed** — extensible without modification of stable code paths?
- **Liskov / Interface Segregation / Dependency Inversion** — apply where relevant.
- **Architecture documents** — does the implementation respect the layering, dependency direction, and integration points specified in `architecture_doc_paths`?

For each violation: `file:line`, rule, evidence (code excerpt), and a concrete refactor.

### Step 5: Production caller chain trace (MANDATORY for service-contract changes)

If the diff modifies a service signature, factory, DI wire-up, or any function that production code calls into, **walk the chain end-to-end before signing off.** The most common dead-on-arrival miss is that the diff *compiles* and *unit tests pass* but the production caller cannot actually reach the new code path. Examples that have slipped past CR:

- `vision_client=None` hardcoded in a factory while the feature's whole point was to use vision.
- A repository query that compiles to `WHERE source_page_id IS NULL` against a non-nullable column — always 0 rows.
- A caller passing threshold `0.6` while the service hardcodes `0.7`.

For every changed service contract, name in the report:

1. **Entrypoint** — the production handler / route / CLI command / scheduled job that drives this code in production.
2. **Call chain** — each intermediate `file:line` from entrypoint → leaf.
3. **Argument shape at each hop** — the actual values production passes (not the kwargs a helper-test prefers to use).

If you cannot complete the chain in a 5-line trace, the diff is dead on arrival. **FAIL with finding type ARCH/wiring** and require the developer to add an end-to-end test that calls from the production entrypoint. Reuse the greps from Step 2 — same searches, different question (callers, not duplicates).

Return the chains in `production_caller_chains`.

### Step 6: Idiomatic language review

Apply the language's community-standard patterns.

Use `../context/code-review-reference.md` for the language-specific checklist.

Replace non-idiomatic constructs with community-standard alternatives. Show before/after when the change isn't obvious.

### Step 7: Complexity & size hotspots

Use tooling where available. See `../context/code-review-reference.md` for example commands.

Flag using `complexity_thresholds` (defaults: cyclomatic 10, file 500 lines, function 50 lines, deep nesting ≥4):

- Functions over the cyclomatic threshold.
- Files over the file-line threshold (guide, not hard rule — justify or recommend splitting).
- Functions over the function-line threshold (guide — same).
- Deep nesting.

Pre-existing complexity above threshold is a **non-blocker** (triage) unless the task substantially expanded it. New complexity above threshold is a **blocker**.

### Step 8: Tests review (design, not execution)

You don't run tests — QA does that. But you do review **test design**:

- **Coverage touchpoints** — does each AC have at least one test case?
- **Boundary conditions** — does the test set include min/max/empty/null/zero/negative-one for each input?
- **Negative cases** — what should NOT happen, and is there a test asserting it?
- **Flaky-risk** — tests with timing, random data, network, real time, or shared state.
- **Mock discipline** — tests that only assert the mock was called are not testing real behavior.
- **Missing cases** — given the AC, list any boundary or negative case the test set doesn't cover.

Findings here are typically non-blockers (triaged as test-debt), unless a critical AC has zero tests at all.

### Step 8.5: Counter-factual test check (per AC, MANDATORY)

For each item in `acceptance_criteria`, point at the specific test(s) that cover it and answer one question:

> Would this test fail against the prior buggy code (or against an empty implementation that only satisfies type signatures)?

If the answer is **"no"**, the AC is not actually covered. The test asserts trivia — the class exists, the method returned without raising, the mock was called — but does not exercise the AC's truth condition. Examples that have slipped past CR and were caught only at UAT:

- AC said *"raise when both tiers <3 entries"* — the only test covered tier-2 = 0 (empty), never 1 or 2; a buggy implementation that just truthy-checked the list would pass.
- AC said *"orchestrator raises X when source_blocks empty"* — only the exception class's constructor was tested; the gate path was never exercised.
- AC tested the happy path with kwargs the production caller never actually passes (see Step 5).

For each AC, return one entry in `counter_factual_per_ac`:

```
{
  ac_id: "AC-X",
  covering_test: "test_name at file:line",
  rationale_or_blocker_flag: "[short rationale why this test would fail against the wrong implementation]"
}
```

If you cannot answer, the AC is a non-blocker triage at minimum (developer adds coverage). If the AC is critical and has no test driving the boundary named in the AC text, it is a **blocker** (set rationale_or_blocker_flag accordingly).

### Step 9: Risk hotspots

Identify and flag:

- Complexity concentrations (a single function holding most of the diff's logic).
- Tight coupling (one module reaching into another's internals).
- Performance bottlenecks (N+1 queries, unnecessary loops, blocking I/O on hot paths).
- Security boundaries (input validation, parameterized queries, secrets handling, injection surfaces).
- Concurrency issues (shared state, lock ordering, goroutine leaks).
- I/O boundary risks (network, file, DB calls without timeouts/error handling).

For each: severity, evidence, and a concrete mitigation.

### Step 10: Independent multi-angle review

Use the host's independent multi-angle review procedure and its capability ladder. The host
runs native Workflow, or six dispatched angle reviewers and a consolidator,
or a validated comparable CLI only when dispatch is unavailable. A failed or
partial six-angle run is INCOMPLETE and cannot be replaced by a single CLI
pass. Manual diagnostics are not an unattended pass. Do not launch a separate
nested Codex craft review. Angle reviewers produce evidence only; the parent
retains the lease, heartbeats, transitions and result ingestion.

Freeze the complete scope, including unaccepted predecessor changes. Bind the
review to the candidate tree, diff hash, manifest and accepted plan revision.
Record runner mode, completed specialists, consolidation, actual reviewer model,
fallback reason, base commit and diff path. Record requested and verified
effective execution identity separately; explicitly mark unknown identity.
If scope or candidate changes, obtain fresh evidence. Preserve disagreements;
justify any downgraded blocker with evidence that it does not mask a test bug,
wiring gap or regressed obligation. Incomplete evidence cannot pass.

### Step 11: Quality rubric (0-5 per dimension)

For each modified file, score 0-5 with a one-sentence justification each. Return in `rubric_scores_per_file`:

- **(a) Readability** — names, structure, flow.
- **(b) Maintainability** — DRY, modularity, clear responsibilities.
- **(c) Performance** — sensible algorithms, no obvious bottlenecks.
- **(d) Testability** — pure functions, dependency injection, seams.
- **(e) Standards Compliance** — adherence to cited standards docs.

Average ≤ 3 for any dimension is a non-blocker triage; ≤ 2 is a blocker.

### Step 12: Triage labeling

For every finding, label it:

| Label | Definition | Goes to |
|---|---|---|
| **Blocker** | AC violation, security flaw, broken contract, missing standards-required behavior, severe complexity in new code | `blockers` — host fails the review |
| **Non-blocker** | Real craft issue but doesn't violate a hard contract — pre-existing complexity, missing nice-to-have test, suggestable refactor, minor reuse opportunity | `non_blockers_to_triage` — host routes through the triage skill's decision tree |
| **Nit** | Stylistic preference, opinion-level, debatable | `nits` — host puts in report only |

The host is responsible for routing every entry in `non_blockers_to_triage` through the triage skill's decision tree (fix-now gate, duplicate search, type classification) before creating any entity — severity ("non-blocker") is not entity type. The fix-now gate may resolve a finding in place with no entity created at all; that counts as triaged. Only findings the decision tree resolves as a maintainability/performance/architectural cost that can safely wait become tech-debt; others may become a bug, task, question, or change instead. If the host's triage attempt fails for any reason (e.g., the target entity is in a state that doesn't accept new children, or the run is unattended and the decision tree's confirmation step has no one to answer it), it falls back to a feature-level note — that's a host concern, not a craft concern.

**Stable fingerprint.** Compute `fingerprint` for every `non_blockers_to_triage` entry from inputs that survive a line-number shift across review rounds: the file path relative to the repo root, the `rule` category, and a short normalized slug of the finding's defect (lowercase, non-alphanumeric collapsed to `-`, e.g. `dry-duplicated-catch-block`) — never the raw line number, which drifts. Format: `<relative_file_path>#<rule>#<slug>`. The triage skill's duplicate search (Step 3 of its own decision tree) checks existing entities for this exact string before creating a new one; if found, it links/updates that entity instead of filing a second one for the same recurring finding.

## Required output structure (report content)

Write the report to `code_review_report_path`. All sections required:

### A. Executive summary (≤ 10 lines)

- What the diff accomplishes (1-2 lines).
- Overall risk level.
- Verdict: **PASS** / **PASS-with-triage** / **FAIL (blocker)**.
- Counts: blockers / non-blockers triaged / nits.

### B. Findings table

| id | severity | file:line | rule | diagnosis | evidence | correction |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | blocker | path/to/file.py:42 | DRY | duplicates `existing_helper()` | `existing_helper()` at lib/helpers.py:18 | replace with import + call |

`rule` is one of: `DRY`, `SRP`, `OCP`, `YAGNI`, `ARCH`, `CODING-STANDARD`, `IDIOM`, `COMPLEXITY`, `RISK`, `TESTS`.

### C. Reuse opportunities

For each duplicated logic: existing symbol + `file:line` + diff-ready refactor.

### D. Standards crosswalk

| finding id | standards doc | section | quoted clause |
|---|---|---|---|

If no standards doc covers a finding, mark "opinion-only" and downgrade severity.

### E. Tests review

- Coverage assessment (which ACs have tests, which don't).
- Missing boundary cases.
- Flaky-risk tests.
- Mock-discipline issues.
- **Counter-factual table** (one row per AC, from `counter_factual_per_ac`).

### F. Quality rubric

| file | readability | maintainability | performance | testability | standards | notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

### G. Risk hotspots

Short list, one per category that applies.

### G2. Independent review evidence

Record the Step 10 metadata, scope, findings, disagreements and downgrade justifications.

### H. Production caller chains

From `production_caller_chains` (Step 5). One sub-section per service-contract change in the diff.

### I. Triage summary

- Blockers: list — must fix before QA.
- Non-blockers triaged: list (from `non_blockers_to_triage`) — host routes each through the triage skill's decision tree and includes, per finding, either the resulting entity key (bug, task, question, change, or tech-debt) or "fixed inline" if the decision tree's fix-now gate resolved it without filing one.
- Nits: bullet list, no action.

### J. Verdict

- **PASS** — no findings of any severity.
- **PASS-with-triage** — no blockers; non-blockers exist (host triages).
- **FAIL** — one or more blockers; task returns to development.

For the full report skeleton, see `../context/code-review-reference.md`.

## Decision rules

### Block (request changes) when:

- AC violation that would let the wrong behavior ship.
- Security vulnerability (injection, XSS, auth bypass, secret exposure).
- Architecture violation against a cited section of an architecture doc.
- Duplicated logic of meaningful size without a stated reason.
- New non-idiomatic construct on a critical path.
- Tests have **zero** coverage of a critical AC.
- Performance regression on a documented hot path.
- New code with cyclomatic complexity over the project threshold.
- **Service-contract change without a complete production caller-chain trace** (Step 5).
- **An AC whose only covering test passes against an empty/buggy implementation** (Step 8.5 counter-factual fails for a critical AC).
- **New `except Exception` (or other catch-all: bare `except:`, `catch (Throwable)`, `} catch {}`) introduced by the diff without a stated diagnose-layer justification per the project's error-handling standard.
- **A review blocker downgraded without the Step 10 evidence-based justification.**

### Do **NOT** block for:

- Stylistic preferences within acceptable bounds.
- Pre-existing issues the task didn't substantially worsen (triage instead).
- Minor optimizations that don't affect correctness.
- Naming nits when project standards are met.
- Missing nice-to-have test cases (triage instead).
- **File-organization preferences** (e.g., "split this 700-line test file into smaller groups"). Route through the triage skill's decision tree only if there is concrete maintainability impact; otherwise drop entirely.
- **Micro-style violations on tiny static-data paths** — e.g., a single sync `Path.read_text()` for a small static prompt file inside an `async def`. Async-purity / hot-path rules apply on hot paths; on a 200-byte one-shot read at module init they are nits, not findings.

### Always prefer:

- Minimal, localized fixes over broad rewrites.
- Concrete code in the correction, not vague guidance.
- Acknowledging good work when it's there.

## Self-verification (before returning)

- [ ] Every blocker cites a specific PRD requirement, standards section, or named risk.
- [ ] Every non-blocker has a clear `file_line` + `fingerprint` + `summary` + `fix_suggestion` in `non_blockers_to_triage` so the host can file it without re-reading the report, and reruns link the same entity instead of duplicating it.
- [ ] Reuse search was actually performed (cite at least one grep result, even if "no duplicates found").
- [ ] Standards crosswalk cites real sections from real docs (no fabricated citations).
- [ ] Quality rubric scores are justified with evidence, not vibes.
- [ ] **Independent review completed and findings are incorporated**; incomplete evidence cannot pass.
- [ ] **For each service-contract change in the diff, `production_caller_chains` names entrypoint → call chain → leaf** (Step 5).
- [ ] **`counter_factual_per_ac` has one entry per AC** (Step 8.5) — flag missing coverage as blocker on critical ACs.
- [ ] **Any downgraded blocker has an evidence-based justification** (Step 10).
- [ ] Verdict matches findings: PASS only if zero findings of any severity; PASS-with-triage if no blockers; FAIL otherwise.
- [ ] Report file exists at `code_review_report_path`.

## Edge cases

- **PRD ambiguous or contradictory** — flag explicitly in report; downgrade related findings; do not invent intent.
- **Standards docs conflict** — note the conflict; do not pick a winner without escalation.
- **Diff is tiny (< 50 lines)** — skip rubric scoring; produce a 4-line report.
- **Diff is huge (> 2000 lines)** — recommend splitting in the verdict; review what fits in attention budget; explicitly mark unreviewed regions.
- **No standards doc exists** — every "standards violation" downgrades to opinion-level; recommend running a standards-generation pass.

## Loop-guard awareness

If `prior_rejection_count >= 1` for this task at code review, **escalate to the user instead of issuing another rejection** — re-rejecting the same code on the same grounds is a loop. The host is responsible for halting the dispatch; the craft simply notes loop-guard state in the report.

## Tone

- Be specific and actionable. Vague feedback ("improve error handling") is not a review.
- Acknowledge good work when it's there — not as flattery, but because reinforcement matters.
- Be direct about blockers; constructive about non-blockers.
- Provide learning context for non-obvious feedback (link to the standards section or pattern doc).
- Respect the developer's effort. The goal is better code shipped, not a higher rejection count.


READ:
(1) Entity specification at docs/plan/E07/E07-F01/E07-F01.md for acceptance criteria
(2) Applicable feature spec or accepted repair plan for architecture decisions and requirements
(3) Applicable test plan or coverage index for expected assertions
(4) Freeze the complete review scope against the accepted baseline, including all unaccepted predecessor changes, staged and unstaged changes and relevant untracked files. Record base, candidate identity, complete file manifest and diff; never assume the last commit is the whole scope.
(5) CLAUDE.md for coding standards

REVIEW:
- [ ] Changes match entity scope (no unrelated modifications)
- [ ] Code follows CLAUDE.md patterns (service layer, error handling, etc.)
- [ ] Architecture aligns with accepted decisions
- [ ] No hardcoded values that should be configurable
- [ ] Error handling follows project conventions
- [ ] No security vulnerabilities (injection, XSS, etc.)
- [ ] Acceptance criteria from the entity specification verified against implementation
- [ ] TDD compliance — tests from the applicable test plan or coverage index are implemented and passing

PROMPT-ONLY SCOPE:
- For embedded prompt, skill, template, or documentation-only changes, verify
  rendering, include resolution, golden updates, and newly documented file
  references.
- Review policy wording against the specification. Do not require wording
  mutation tests, decision tables, or caller-path contracts unless the change
  modifies deterministic runtime behavior.

REVIEW OUTPUT POLICY (applies to the report below):
- If there are zero findings of any kind, write a compact success artifact instead of a full narrative report.
- Compact success artifact must include: verdict; exact scope reviewed; commands/tests/checks run (summarized, not raw logs); counts or totals reviewed; duration if known; explicit line `0 defects found`.
- Do NOT echo full checklists, full coverage matrices, or raw command logs in a zero-finding artifact.
- Summarize command output. Include raw log paths only when failures or findings exist.
- If there is ANY blocker, rejection, failed command/test, broken contract, missing coverage, non-blocking observation, or other finding, write the full detailed report with file paths, evidence, affected AC/TC/CONTRACT/I-##/X-## IDs, defect-class statements where the prompt asks for them, and concrete fix guidance.
- If this prompt uses review-finding notes, emit them only when findings exist.
- Console output stays terse:
  - zero-finding PASS/APPROVED -> one-line summary plus report path
  - any finding -> one-line fail/triage summary plus report path


PRODUCE code review report at `docs/review/E07/E07-F01/code-review-<timestamp>-E07-F01.md`:
- If zero findings: compact PASS artifact only
  - Verdict: PASS
  - Scope reviewed: task spec, feature context, diff, and implementation surface
  - Checks run: review checklist and tests/commands referenced, summarized
  - AC count reviewed
  - Duration if known
  - `0 defects found`
- If any blocker or non-blocking observation exists: full detailed report
  - Verdict: PASS or FAIL
  - Findings with file paths, evidence, failed commands/tests if any, affected ACs, and concrete fix guidance
  - AC verification status


For re-review, run the full-class re-verification procedure in
`skills/quality/workflows/defect-class-sweep.md` (if the bundle is not extracted, read it with `shark skill get quality workflows/defect-class-sweep.md`). Its evidence-based recurrence
classification and the project's retry limits govern escalation; do not treat
a raw review round count alone as recurrence. Preserve accepted dispositions
unless new evidence invalidates them.


The fail outcome's role is `route_rework`. Return pass only when all checks and complete current evidence pass; otherwise choose the earliest invalidated stage as above. The parent applies the outcome.

FINAL RESPONSE — STRUCTURED GATE RESULT (T-E34-F05, `result_contract: gate_result_v1`):
Do NOT end your response with a `RECOMMENDED OUTCOME: <key>` line, a `PARENT NOTE: <text>` line, or a bare `<entity-id> -> <status> --reason "<why>"` kickback line — those are the legacy free-form directive grammar and this step does not use it. Instead, the ENTIRE trimmed final response must be exactly one worker-control envelope (`kind: final`), with a nested `gate_result` payload:

```
{
  "kind": "final",
  "recommended_outcome": "<the outcome key you are recommending, e.g. pass or fail>",
  "evidence": [],
  "gate_result": {
    "schema_version": 1,
    "summary": "<one paragraph: verdict and why>",
    "findings": [ ... ],
    "kickbacks": [ ... ],
    "no_kickback_reason": "<only when required — see below>"
  }
}
```

The nested `gate_result` shape is validated and typed in `internal/gateresult`
(see its doc comment for the full field list — `findings[]`,
`kickbacks[]`, `remediation_sweeps[]`, `change_impacts[]`); this note does
not restate it. Two rules this step's outcomes commonly need:

- Whether `kickbacks` must be empty or non-empty for a given outcome depends
  on that outcome's configured semantic role (`outcome_roles`, visible via
  `shark get E07-F01 --json` or `shark next E07-F01 --json` ->
  `result_contract`/`outcome_roles`), not on this prompt's own text:
  - role `success` (typically `pass`): zero `kickbacks`, and no `findings`
    left with `disposition: open` or `severity_conflict`.
  - role `kickback_rework` (a per-item rework outcome): at least one
    `kickbacks` entry (`entity_key`, `target_status`, `reason`) — one per
    item that must reopen, replacing the legacy
    `<entity-id> -> <status> --reason "<why>"` line format.
  - role `route_rework` (this step's own main entity re-routes as a whole):
    zero `kickbacks` — the configured route itself is the rework path; do
    not also emit per-item kickbacks for this role.
  - roles `blocked`/`hold`/`cancelled`: either at least one kickback or a
    non-empty `no_kickback_reason` explaining why none applies.
- A blocking finding you are reporting (not fixing) must carry
  `disposition: open` (or `severity_conflict` when reviewers disagree);
  every other GateResult field constraint is enforced the same way
  regardless of which role this outcome carries.

Write your full narrative report to the file this step already directs
(unchanged); the envelope's `summary` is a compact verdict statement, not a
duplicate of that report.
